// ora-mod-po — generated by tools/merge.js: src/ora-mod-po-*.js plus matching questions from the Oracle role banks (tools/modules.js).
QB['ora-mod-po'] = (QB['ora-mod-po'] || []).concat([["Procurement","How would you architect source-to-pay for a large enterprise on Fusion?","- **Supplier lifecycle**: registration, qualification, risk screening, performance.\n- **Sourcing** events and **contracts** with clause library.\n- **Catalogs** and agreements; **guided buying** in Self Service Procurement.\n- **Approvals** by budget owners and category.\n- **Receiving/acceptance** for goods and services.\n- **AP automation** (e-invoicing, IDR, matching).\n- **Spend analytics** and savings tracking.\n- **Integrations**: supplier networks, risk providers, e-signature.","D"],["Procurement","How do you design procurement for services (contractors, SOWs) in Oracle?","- **Fixed-price** and **rate-based** services lines; **complex work** (progress payments, retainage) for construction/projects.\n- **Service receipts** (amount or quantity based) — receipt by requester or self-billing.\n- **Contingent workforce** may use a VMS (e.g. Fieldglass/Beeline) integrated with Fusion for POs and invoices.\n- **Approvals** by project/cost center owner.\n- Matching options: 2-way or 3-way with service acceptance.","D"],["Procurement","Explain the Procure-to-Pay flow and the main documents.","- **Requisition** — internal request (Self Service Procurement), approved.\n- **Purchase order** — created from the requisition (manually or automatically), approved and sent to the supplier.\n- **Receipt** — goods/services received (Receiving), optionally inspected and delivered to inventory or expense.\n- **Invoice** — matched in AP to PO and receipt.\n- **Payment** — AP payment run.\n\nAccounting: receipt → Dr Inventory/Expense, Cr Accrual; invoice → Dr Accrual, Cr AP Liability; payment → Dr Liability, Cr Cash.","B"],["Procurement","What types of purchase orders are there?","- **Standard PO** — one-time purchase with specific items, quantities, prices and dates.\n- **Blanket Purchase Agreement (BPA)** — agreed prices for items over a period, without fixed quantities. You release orders against it.\n- **Contract Purchase Agreement (CPA)** — agreed terms with a supplier but no specific items; standard POs reference it.\n- **Planned PO** (EBS) — committed quantities with tentative dates; scheduled releases.\n\nIn Fusion: Standard PO, Blanket Agreement and Contract Agreement.","B"],["Procurement","How does a requisition get converted into a purchase order automatically?","In Fusion:\n- The requisition line references an **agreement** (BPA) or catalog item with an agreed price and supplier.\n- Requisition is approved.\n- **Automated buyer processing** (configured in Procurement setup) creates the PO automatically if: the line has a valid source agreement, the supplier site is active, and the agreement allows automatic ordering.\n- The PO goes through approval (or is auto-approved within limits) and is communicated to the supplier.\n\nLines without a source go to the **buyer's Process Requisitions** work area for manual conversion.","B"],["Procurement","How are purchasing approvals configured in Fusion?","- Approvals use **BPM** rules via **Manage Requisition/Purchasing Document Approvals**.\n- Rules can be based on: amount, category, cost center, project, supervisor hierarchy, position or job level, or approval groups.\n- Example: requisitions under $5,000 → supervisor; over $5,000 → supervisor then department head; IT categories → IT approver.\n- Test rules thoroughly with different amounts and categories; check for \"no approver found\" errors.","B"],["Procurement","What is a procurement catalog?","Catalogs let requesters shop like an online store in Self Service Procurement.\n- **Local catalog** — items and prices loaded into Fusion (from BPAs or catalog uploads).\n- **Punchout catalog** — the user goes to the supplier's website, picks items, and returns a cart.\n- **Informational catalog** — instructions or links (e.g. \"how to order business cards\").\n- **Content zones** control which users see which catalogs.\n- **Smart forms** for non-catalog requests with guided fields.","D"],["Procurement","How do you onboard a new supplier in Oracle Fusion?","- **Supplier registration** — the supplier registers through the supplier portal or is entered by procurement.\n- **Approval** of the registration (rules).\n- Create the **supplier**: tax ID, addresses, **sites** per procurement BU, **site assignments** to client BUs, bank account (with verification), contacts, payment terms/method.\n- **Supplier qualification** questionnaires if needed (e.g. diversity, compliance).\n- Control who can change bank details (a common fraud risk).","B"],["Procurement","What is sourcing (RFQ/auction) in Oracle Procurement?","**Oracle Sourcing** creates negotiations to get competitive bids:\n- **RFI** — request for information.\n- **RFQ** — request for quotes.\n- **Auction** — suppliers see rankings and rebid.\n- Steps: create the negotiation (lines, requirements, terms), invite suppliers, collect responses, **score and analyze**, **award**, and create a **PO or agreement** from the award.\n\nIt improves pricing and creates an audit trail for supplier selection.","B"],["Procurement","What happens when you need to change an approved purchase order?","- Create a **change order** (Fusion) — the PO opens a new revision.\n- Changes like price, quantity or dates may need **re-approval** (based on rules).\n- The revised PO is communicated to the supplier.\n- Some changes are blocked after receipt or invoicing (e.g. you can't reduce quantity below received).\n- **Cancel** or **close** POs when no longer needed (Closed for Receiving, Closed for Invoicing, Finally Closed).","B"],["Procurement","What are the PO close statuses?","- **Open** — normal.\n- **Closed for receiving** — can still be invoiced (e.g. received within tolerance).\n- **Closed for invoicing** — can still be received.\n- **Closed** — both received and invoiced; can be reopened.\n- **Finally closed** — permanent; releases encumbrance; cannot be reopened.\n- **Canceled** — nothing more can happen on it.\n\nReceipt and invoice close tolerances drive automatic closure.","D"],["Procurement","What is a Procurement agent and why does it matter?","**Procurement agents** are buyers set up with access to specific **procurement BUs** and actions (manage requisitions, POs, agreements, suppliers, sourcing).\n- Defined in **Manage Procurement Agents**.\n- Controls which documents a buyer can see and act on (own documents vs all documents in the BU).\n- Without an agent setup, a user with the buyer role still can't create POs in that BU — a common support issue.","D"],["Procurement","What is the difference between a requisitioning BU, procurement BU and sold-to legal entity?","- **Requisitioning BU** — where the requester works; owns the requisition and the expense/charge accounts.\n- **Procurement BU** — the purchasing team that creates and manages the PO and supplier relationship; can serve many requisitioning BUs.\n- **Sold-to legal entity** — the legal entity that is financially liable for the purchase (and appears on the PO).\n- The **bill-to BU** processes the invoice in AP.\n\nThis model supports centralized procurement for many BUs in Fusion.","D"],["Procurement","What is the difference between a requisition, a purchase order and an agreement?","- **Requisition** — an internal request to buy; needs approval.\n- **Purchase order** — the legal order to the supplier with quantities, prices and dates.\n- **Agreement** (blanket or contract) — negotiated terms/prices for a period; POs are created against it.","B"],["Procurement","How do you fix invoice matching problems on blanket POs with many receipts?","- Symptom: AP staff must pick specific receipts, often stale ones, and invoices go on hold.\n- Option: change the **invoice match option** on the BPA/PO schedules from **Receipt** to **Purchase Order** — still **3-way** (receipt quantity is checked in total), but AP matches at PO level.\n- Update **document style/agreement defaults** so new releases default correctly; consider **mass update** for open POs.\n- Check impacts: price variance analysis by receipt, accrual clearing, reports.\n- Train receivers to receive promptly so totals are right.\n- Pilot with a few high-volume suppliers before rolling out.","D"],["Procurement","What KPIs would you set up for procurement?","- **Spend under management** / on-contract %.\n- **PO cycle time** (requisition to PO).\n- **Touchless PO %** (auto-created POs).\n- **Savings** (negotiated vs baseline).\n- **Supplier performance**: on-time delivery, quality rejects.\n- **Maverick spend**.\n- **Buyer productivity**.\n\nUse OTBI/FDI dashboards; baseline before go-live.","B"],["Procurement","How would you roll out Self Service Procurement to all employees?","- **Catalogs first**: top categories by spend in local/punchout catalogs.\n- **Smart forms** for common non-catalog requests (services, IT).\n- **Approval rules** simple and based on HR hierarchy + amount.\n- **Training**: short videos, in-app guidance; super users per department.\n- **Pilot** with a few departments; measure catalog adoption and approval times.\n- **Communications** explaining \"how to buy\".\n- Track **catalog spend %** and requisition cycle time.","B"],["Procurement","How would you design supplier onboarding with risk and compliance checks?","- **Registration** via supplier portal with required documents (W-9/W-8, insurance, certifications).\n- **Supplier Qualification** questionnaires for risk (financial, ESG, cyber, sanctions).\n- **Third-party screening** (sanctions, D&B risk scores) via integration.\n- **Approval** by procurement, finance (bank details verification), and compliance.\n- **Bank account verification** process (callback) to prevent fraud.\n- Periodic **requalification** and certificate expiry alerts.","D"],["Procurement","How do you manage contracts in Oracle Procurement?","- **Procurement Contracts** for authoring with **clause library**, templates and deviations review.\n- Link contracts to **agreements** and **POs** (contract terms on purchase documents).\n- **Approval workflow** including legal for non-standard clauses.\n- **Deliverables** tracking (obligations) with reminders.\n- **Repository** with renewals and expirations alerts.\n- E-signature integration.","D"],["Procurement","How would you improve requisition-to-PO cycle time in Fusion?","- Catalog and agreement coverage for top categories → **automatic PO creation**.\n- **Smart forms** for common non-catalog requests.\n- Simpler **approval rules**.\n- **AI requisition assistant** for requesters.\n- Measure and review weekly by category and BU.","D"],["Procurement","How do blanket agreements with price breaks work?","- Create a **blanket purchase agreement** with items, prices and **price breaks** (by quantity, ship-to org, or dates).\n- Requisitions and POs that reference the agreement get the right price automatically.\n- **Cumulative** price breaks consider total quantity released so far; **non-cumulative** look at each release.\n- Agreement **amount limits** and **effective dates** control usage.\n- Enable **automatic sourcing** so requisitions pick up the agreement.","D"],["Procurement","How do you configure supplier-managed price changes through the supplier portal?","- Enable suppliers to request **agreement changes** via the **Supplier Portal** (change orders on agreements).\n- Changes go through **approval** by the buyer (and rules by % change).\n- Supplier must be registered with portal access and the right roles.\n- Once approved, the new prices apply to new orders from the effective date.\n- Audit trail kept on the agreement revision history.","D"],["Procurement","What are the key Procurement setups you configure for a new procurement BU?","- **Business unit** with Procurement and Requisitioning functions and **service provider** relationships.\n- **Procurement agents** and their access.\n- **Common options** (default currency, line types, document numbering).\n- **Configure procurement business function** (default ship-to, buyer assignment, receipt/invoice options).\n- **Document styles**, **approvals**, **catalogs and content zones**.\n- **Supplier site assignments** for the BU.\n- **Purchasing categories** and **buyer assignment rules**.","B"],["Procurement","How does supplier acknowledgment of purchase orders work?","- On the PO, set **acknowledgment required** (document, schedule-level) with a due date.\n- Supplier acknowledges (accept/reject, propose changes) in the **Supplier Portal** or via B2B.\n- Buyer reviews proposed changes and accepts/rejects them, creating a change order.\n- Monitor pending acknowledgments with OTBI/infolets.\n\nUseful for long-lead or critical items where you need the supplier's committed dates.","D"],["B2B","How do you exchange POs and invoices electronically with suppliers in Fusion?","- **Collaboration Messaging Framework (CMK)** — Fusion's B2B layer.\n- Supports **OAGIS** XML and **cXML**; EDI (X12 850/855/856/810) usually via a **B2B service provider** or OIC + an EDI translator.\n- Setup: trading partners, **collaboration documents**, delivery methods, and **supplier/customer associations**.\n- Messages: outbound PO, inbound PO acknowledgment, inbound ASN, inbound invoice.\n- Monitor in the **Collaboration Messaging History**.","D"],["Procurement","What is spend authorization or sourcing compliance and why does it matter?","It ensures purchases go through **approved suppliers and agreements**:\n- **Approved Supplier List (ASL)** and **sourcing rules** point requisitions to contracted suppliers.\n- Catalog-first buying through Self Service Procurement.\n- **Non-catalog requests** route for extra approval.\n- Reports on off-contract spend (\"maverick spend\").\n\nBenefits: better prices, fewer suppliers to manage, compliance with policies.","B"],["Procurement","How does Supplier Qualification Management work?","- **Questions** and **questionnaires** (e.g. certifications, insurance, ESG, diversity).\n- **Qualifications** and **assessments** — scored evaluations of a supplier for a business relationship.\n- **Initiatives** send questionnaires to many suppliers at once.\n- Results feed supplier eligibility (e.g. only qualified suppliers can be awarded in sourcing).\n- Expiry tracking for certificates.","D"],["Procurement","How do you handle requisitions that fail automatic PO creation?","- Check the requisition line in **Process Requisitions** — the reason is usually shown (no source agreement, supplier site inactive, agreement expired, price/UOM mismatch, missing procurement BU access).\n- Fix the root data: renew the agreement, add the site assignment, correct the UOM conversion.\n- Re-submit or let the buyer create the PO manually.\n- Track repeat failures in a report so the root cause (e.g. expired agreements) is fixed before it hits users.","B"],["Procurement","How do you set up buyer assignment rules?","- Define **Manage Procurement Agents** for buyers.\n- **Buyer assignment rules** route requisition lines to buyers by **category**, BU, requester location, or amount.\n- Lines without a match go to a default buyer.\n- Review workload balance periodically.","B"],["Procurement","What are line types and why do they matter?","- **Goods** — quantity × price, received in units.\n- **Services/fixed price** — amount-based, received by amount.\n- **Rate-based** (temp labor) — rate × hours.\n- Line type drives receiving, matching (quantity vs amount) and accounting. Wrong line type = matching problems in AP.","D"],["Procurement","How would you transform procurement with digital tools on Oracle?","- **Guided buying** (catalogs, smart forms) to increase compliance.\n- **Sourcing** automation and **contract** lifecycle management.\n- **Supplier risk and ESG** integrated in onboarding and monitoring.\n- **Touchless P2P** (auto-PO, e-invoicing).\n- **AI agents** for supplier queries, contract analysis and negotiation preparation.\n- **Spend analytics** with classification.\n- KPIs: spend under management, savings, cycle times.","D"]]);
